{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.MassGapGm","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Archimedean.MassGapGm","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.GroundRing","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.TransferMatrix","lean:module:QOU.ClassicalLimitObstruction","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Polynomial.Laurent","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Linarith","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.FieldSimp","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Positivity"],"declarations":22,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.descartes_massGap_and_kummer_5","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.descartes_massGap_and_kummer_5","kind":"theorem","line":304,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/MassGapGm.lean#L304-L310","doc":"The Descartes mass gap and Kummer obstruction: gap > 0\nand obstruction persists at level 5 (since 5 ∤ -6). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.massGap_and_kummer_from_non_torsion","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.massGap_and_kummer_from_non_torsion","kind":"theorem","line":287,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/MassGapGm.lean#L287-L302","doc":"**Bridge theorem**: the mass gap bound and the Kummer obstruction\nshare the same torsion-avoidance condition.\n\nFor `q₀ > 1` and `q₀ ∉ μ_∞`:\n- `massGapReal q₀ > 0`  (mass gap positive)\n- Kummer obstruction persists at all levels `n ∤ χ(A)`\n\nThe Kummer divisibility is proved in `ClassicalLimitObstruction.lean`\nvia `kummer_obstruction_divisibility`.  Here we combine it with\nthe mass gap positivity. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.pp_alternating_eigenvalue_neg_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.pp_alternating_eigenvalue_neg_one","kind":"theorem","line":267,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/MassGapGm.lean#L267-L272","doc":"The alternating eigenvalue of the pp crossing is `−1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.gs_nf_determines_spectrum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.gs_nf_determines_spectrum","kind":"theorem","line":260,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/MassGapGm.lean#L260-L265","doc":"GS normal form uniqueness implies spectral well-definedness. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.FullRepTransferMatrix.spectralProduct","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.FullRepTransferMatrix.spectralProduct","kind":"def","line":253,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/MassGapGm.lean#L253-L256","doc":"The spectral product (product of all eigenvalues). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.FullRepTransferMatrix.fromCrossingMatrix","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.FullRepTransferMatrix.fromCrossingMatrix","kind":"def","line":246,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/MassGapGm.lean#L246-L251","doc":"Construct from a 2×2 crossing matrix. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.FullRepTransferMatrix.lamAlt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.FullRepTransferMatrix.lamAlt","kind":"def","line":244,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/MassGapGm.lean#L244-L244","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.FullRepTransferMatrix.T_std","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.FullRepTransferMatrix.T_std","kind":"def","line":243,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/MassGapGm.lean#L243-L243","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.FullRepTransferMatrix.lamSym","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.FullRepTransferMatrix.lamSym","kind":"def","line":242,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/MassGapGm.lean#L242-L242","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.FullRepTransferMatrix.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.FullRepTransferMatrix.mk","kind":"ctor","line":241,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/MassGapGm.lean#L241-L241","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.FullRepTransferMatrix","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.FullRepTransferMatrix","kind":"structure","line":232,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/MassGapGm.lean#L232-L244","doc":"The 6×6 transfer matrix for the full representation of `H₃(q)`.\n\nThe Hecke algebra `H₃(q)` has 3 irreducible representations:\n- symmetric `(3)`: dim 1\n- standard `(2,1)`: dim 2\n- alternating `(1³)`: dim 1\n\nThe 6×6 matrix is the block-diagonal direct sum:\n`T₆ = T_sym ⊕ T_std ⊕ T_std ⊕ T_alt` ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.massGapReal_mono","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.massGapReal_mono","kind":"theorem","line":166,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/MassGapGm.lean#L166-L228","doc":"The mass gap is monotonically increasing for `q₀ > 1`.\n\nProof: write `Δ(q) = (q − q⁻¹)(1 − q⁻¹)² = (q² − 1)(q − 1)² / q³`.\nFor `q > 1`: numerator `(q²−1)(q−1)²` is increasing (product of\nincreasing positive terms), denominator `q³` is increasing but slower.\nNet: `Δ(q)` is increasing. We prove this via the derivative test\nreformulated as a discrete comparison. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.massGapReal_at_neg_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.massGapReal_at_neg_one","kind":"theorem","line":162,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/MassGapGm.lean#L162-L164","doc":"The mass gap vanishes at `q₀ = −1` (2-torsion). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.massGapReal_at_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.massGapReal_at_one","kind":"theorem","line":158,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/MassGapGm.lean#L158-L160","doc":"The mass gap vanishes at `q₀ = 1` (classical limit). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.not_root_of_unity_implies_massGap_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.not_root_of_unity_implies_massGap_pos","kind":"theorem","line":146,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/MassGapGm.lean#L146-L156","doc":"The `not_root_of_unity` axiom implies the mass gap is positive.\n\n`q₀ ≥ 1` and `q₀^k ≠ 1` for all `k ≥ 1` forces `q₀ > 1`\n(since `q₀ = 1` gives `1^1 = 1`), so `massGapReal q₀ > 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.massGapReal_ne_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.massGapReal_ne_zero","kind":"theorem","line":141,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/MassGapGm.lean#L141-L144","doc":"For `q₀ > 1`, the mass gap is non-zero. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.massGapReal_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.massGapReal_pos","kind":"theorem","line":123,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/MassGapGm.lean#L123-L139","doc":"For `q₀ > 1`, the mass gap is strictly positive.\n\nProof: `q₀ > 1` implies `q₀⁻¹ < 1` and `q₀ > q₀⁻¹`,\nso both factors are strictly positive. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.massGapReal_eq_zero_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.massGapReal_eq_zero_iff","kind":"theorem","line":91,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/MassGapGm.lean#L91-L121","doc":"The mass gap is zero if and only if `q₀ = 1` or `q₀ = −1`.\n\nProof: factor as `(q₀ − q₀⁻¹)(1 − q₀⁻¹)²`.\n- First factor `q₀ − q₀⁻¹ = (q₀² − 1)/q₀`, zero iff `q₀ = ±1`.\n- Second factor `(1 − q₀⁻¹)² = ((q₀ − 1)/q₀)²`, zero iff `q₀ = 1`.\nProduct is zero iff either factor is zero, i.e. `q₀ ∈ {1, −1}`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.massGapReal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.massGapReal","kind":"def","line":82,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/MassGapGm.lean#L82-L89","doc":"The real-valued mass gap function at `q₀ ∈ ℝ×`:\n`Δ(q₀) = (q₀ − q₀⁻¹)(1 − q₀⁻¹)²`.\n\nThis equals `eval₂(two_massGapBound, q₀)` by the ring\nhomomorphism property of `eval₂`, but is defined directly\nfor ease of proof. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.two_massGapBound_expanded","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.two_massGapBound_expanded","kind":"theorem","line":68,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/MassGapGm.lean#L68-L72","doc":"Expanded form: `2Δ_q = (q − q⁻¹)(1 − q⁻¹)²`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.two_massGapBound_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.two_massGapBound_eq","kind":"theorem","line":65,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/MassGapGm.lean#L65-L66","doc":"The mass gap factors as `HA · hbarQ²`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.two_massGapBound","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.two_massGapBound","kind":"def","line":54,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/MassGapGm.lean#L54-L63","doc":"The **doubled mass gap bound** as an element of `ℤ[q, q⁻¹]`.\n\n`2Δ_q = HA · hbarQ² = (q − q⁻¹)(1 − q⁻¹)²`\n\nThis is a regular function on 𝔾_m.  The factor of 2 keeps us\nin ℤ[q, q⁻¹] (avoiding the ½ in E_cross).\n\nOver ℝ at `q₀ > 1`:\n`2Δ(q₀) = (q₀ − q₀⁻¹)(1 − q₀⁻¹)² > 0` ","signatureChunk":0}]}